{ Welcome to the kings gambit, an aggressive, high-risk opening for players who want to force sharp, tactical positions early. On move two you offer your f pawn to pull his e pawn out of the middle. Start with the king pawn. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Your king pawn takes a square in the middle and opens a road for your light bishop. If he answers with c5 or e6, you are in another opening. [%cal Gf1c4] } (1. d4 { The queen pawn is just as good a start, but it leads to quieter games. } 1... d5 2. c4 { That is the queen gambit, a pawn offered on the other wing. This gambit starts with the king pawn. [%csl Bc4] }) (1. Nf3 { A flexible first move, and a good one. } 1... d5 { A calm queen pawn game follows. Today the king pawn goes first. [%csl Bd5] }) (1. c4 { The English opening: sound, and played from the side. } 1... e5 { A slow, roomy game. The kings gambit needs your king pawn in the middle first. [%csl Be5] }) 1... e5 { He answers with his own king pawn. Now the gambit: push your f pawn two squares and offer it to his e pawn. [%cal Yf2f4] } 2. f4 { That is the kings gambit: you offer your f pawn so that his e pawn leaves the middle, and your d pawn can take its place. [%cal Ge5f4,Gd2d4] } (2. Nf3 { The most played move of all, and a very good one, but it leads to other openings. } 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 { That is the Ruy Lopez, a study of its own. Here your f pawn goes forward instead. [%csl Bb5] }) (2. Nc3 { A good developing move, and the start of the Vienna. } 2... Nf6 { His knight comes out and the game stays calm. Today the f pawn goes forward at once. [%csl Bc3] }) (2. Bc4 { The bishop aims at f7 at once, a fine opening. } 2... Nf6 3. d3 { Solid and quiet: your pawn has to guard e4. Today you give a pawn to open lines instead. [%cal Bd3e4] }) (2. d4 { Opening the middle at once is fine: that is the centre game, another opening. } 2... exd4 { Level, but his pawn took yours, and the kings gambit never happens. [%csl Bd4] }) 2... exf4 { He takes, and his pawn has left the middle. Had his queen checked on h4 instead, your g pawn would block it. Now bring your king knight to f3. [%cal Yg1f3] } 3. Nf3 { The knight guards h4, so his queen cannot check you there. [%cal Gf3h4] } (3. Bc4 { Just as good: that is the bishop gambit, another sound opening. } 3... Qh4+ 4. Kf1 { About level, but his queen check sent your king to f1, and it can never castle now. [%csl Rf1] }) (3. d4 { The pawn takes the middle, which is fine, but h4 is still open to his queen. } 3... Qh4+ 4. Ke2 { Slightly better for him: your king is stuck on e2 and has lost the right to castle for good. [%csl Re2] }) (3. Nc3 { A natural developing move, but it leaves h4 without a guard. } 3... Qh4+ { His queen checks at once, and the best answer walks your king to e2, giving up castling. Slightly better for him. [%cal Rh4e1] }) 3... g5 { He guards his extra pawn: the pawn on g5 holds the one on f4. Hit the guard: your h pawn goes two squares. [%cal Yh2h4,Rg5f4] } 4. h4 { Your h pawn hits g5. If he takes it, your rook on h1 gets an open column. [%cal Gh4g5,Gh1h5] } (4. Bc4 { Popular, but it lets his g pawn jump at your knight. } 4... g4 5. O-O gxf3 6. Qxf3 { A knight for a pawn: the attack is famous, but a whole piece is a lot to give. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rf3] }) (4. d4 { Taking the middle is just as good, but your h pawn stays at home. } 4... g4 5. Ne5 Qh4+ 6. g3 { Slightly better for him: without the h pawn push his queen gets to check on h4, and your king side opens up. [%csl Rg3] }) (4. Nc3 { Just as good: your queen knight comes out first. } 4... d6 { Slightly better for him: he keeps the extra pawn, and his d pawn now guards e5 against your knights. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (4. h3 { Nearly as good: the pawn gets ready to hit g4. } 4... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: he develops, and his pawns still hold f4. [%csl Rf4] }) 4... g4 { His pawn steps past and hits your knight. Do not go home: jump forward to e5. [%cal Yf3e5,Rg4f3] } 5. Ne5 { Out on e5 the knight is safe from his pawns, and it eyes his pawn on g4. [%cal Ge5g4] } (5. Ng5 { The knight jumps at f7, but one pawn push chases it. } 5... h6 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 { A knight for one pawn. His king had to step up, but that is not enough for a whole piece: slightly better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (5. Ng1 { Back home is safe, but slow. } 5... d5 { Slightly better for him: your knight spent two moves going home, and he grabs the middle. [%csl Rg1] }) (5. d4 { The middle can wait: your knight is under attack. } 5... gxf3 6. Qxf3 { His pawn took your knight, and your queen won only a pawn back. Better for him. [%cal Rg4f3] }) 5... Nf6 { His knight comes out and hits e4. Back up your knight first: your d pawn goes two squares. [%cal Yd2d4,Rf6e4] } 6. d4 { The pawn guards your knight on e5 and takes the middle. [%cal Gd4e5] } (6. Bc4 { Just as good, and the most played: the bishop stares at f7. } 6... d5 { Slightly better for him: he strikes back in the middle at once, and a wild fight follows. The pawn to d4 is calmer. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (6. Nxg4 { Taking the pawn back at once is at least as good, maybe better. } 6... Nxe4 7. d3 Ng3 8. Bxf4 { Level: material is even, but his knight on g3 hits your rook in the corner, and the game turns wild. [%cal Rg3h1] }) (6. Nxf7 { A knight for a pawn is too much to give here. } 6... Kxf7 7. d4 { His king stepped up, but you are a piece down for one pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (6. d3 { The pawn guards e4, but your knight on e5 has no support. [%cal Bd3e4] } 6... d6 7. Nc4 Nh5 { Better for him: he keeps the pawn on f4, and his knight on h5 guards it. [%cal Rh5f4] }) (6. Nc3 { The knight guards e4, but nothing guards your knight on e5. [%cal Bc3e4][%csl Re5] } 6... d6 7. Nc4 Nh5 { Better for him: your knight was pushed back, and his knight is one jump from g3, where it would hit your rook. [%cal Rh5g3,Rg3h1] }) 6... d6 { His d pawn kicks your knight. Pull it back to d3, where it hits f4. That hands him your e pawn, a second gambit pawn, but your bishop wins his pawn back next. [%cal Ye5d3,Gd3f4] } 7. Nd3 { From d3 the knight hits f4, the pawn he took on move two. [%cal Gd3f4] } (7. Nxf7 { A knight for a pawn, and his king only steps aside. } 7... Kxf7 8. Bxf4 { You have two pawns for the knight, and his king is safe enough. Better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (7. Nc4 { The knight steps back, but from c4 it does nothing. } 7... Bh6 { Better for him: his bishop now guards f4, and your knight on c4 attacks nothing. [%cal Rh6f4] }) (7. Bxf4 { Taking the pawn first leaves your knight hanging. } 7... dxe5 8. Bxe5 { His d pawn took your knight: you have only two pawns for it. Better for him. [%csl Re5] }) (7. Nxg4 { The knight grabs a pawn, but on g4 it is not safe. } 7... Bxg4 { His bishop takes back: a whole knight for one pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rg4] }) (7. Bb5+ { A check, but it solves nothing. } 7... c6 { His pawn blocks and hits your bishop, and his d pawn still hits your knight: one of them falls. Better for him. [%cal Rc6b5,Rd6e5] }) 7... Nxe4 { He takes the second pawn. Now your dark bishop takes f4 back. [%cal Yc1f4] } 8. Bxf4 { Your bishop takes back f4 and aims at d6. You gave two pawns and won one back: he is a pawn up, and your pieces are out. [%cal Gf4d6] } (8. Qe2 { Just as good: your queen hits his knight first. [%cal Ge2e4] } 8... Qe7 9. Bxf4 Nc6 { Slightly better for him: he is a pawn up, though the queens face each other and your bishop took f4 back. [%csl Gf4] }) (8. Nxf4 { The wrong piece takes: your knight leaves, and h4 is loose. } 8... Be7 9. Bd3 Bxh4+ 10. Kf1 { His bishop took your h pawn with check, and your king had to walk. He is two pawns up: better for him. [%cal Re7h4] }) (8. Nc3 { Developing is natural, but his knight gets a great jump. } 8... Ng3 9. Rh2 Qe7+ 10. Kf2 { Your rook is stuck on h2, your king has walked to f2, and he is two pawns up. Better for him. [%csl Rh2,Rf2] }) (8. Be2 { A quiet move, but it lets his f pawn run. } 8... f3 9. gxf3 Ng3 10. Rh2 { His knight on g3 hits your bishop, and your rook had to hide on h2. Better for him. [%cal Rg3e2] }) 8... Qe7 { His bishop to g7 was the quiet choice. Now his queen faces your king down the e column: if his knight jumps away, it checks and can win your queen. Block with your queen. [%cal Re7e1,Yd1e2] } 9. Qe2 { Your queen blocks the column, so his knight can move without check, and she hits that knight. Against his bishop to g7, your knight or c pawn comes to c3. [%cal Ge2e4] } (9. Be2 { Just as good: the bishop blocks the column instead. } 9... Bg7 { Slightly better for him with his extra pawn, but your bishop shuts the e column, and your queen stays at home. [%cal Ge2e7] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it walks into the trap. } 9... Nxc3+ 10. Kd2 Nxd1 11. Rxd1 { His knight moved with check from his queen and then took yours. A queen for a knight: that is lost. [%cal Re7e1] }) (9. c3 { The pawn guards d4, but the e column is still open. [%cal Bc3d4] } 9... Nxc3+ 10. Be2 Nxd1 { His knight jumped out with check and then ate your queen: lost for you. [%csl Rd1] }) (9. Nd2 { The knight hits his knight, but it is too slow. } 9... Nc3+ 10. Kf2 Nxd1+ { The knight leapt out with check from his queen and then took yours. Lost for you. [%cal Re7e1] }) (9. Be3 { The bishop blocks the column, but it leaves g3 to his knight. } 9... Ng3 10. Kd2 Nxh1 { His knight took your rook in the corner: you are a rook and a pawn down. Better for him. [%csl Rh1] }) 9... Nc6 { His knight comes out and hits d4. Guard it with a pawn. [%cal Rc6d4,Yc2c3] } 10. c3 { The c pawn guards d4, so his knight has nothing to hit. [%cal Gc3d4] } (10. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but now nothing guards d4. [%csl Rd4] } 10... f5 11. O-O-O Nxd4 { His knight took d4: he is two pawns up. Better for him. [%csl Rd4] }) (10. Nd2 { The knight hits his knight, but he swaps it and the queens first. } 10... Nxd2 11. Qxe7+ Bxe7 12. Bxd2 Nxd4 { The queens are gone and his knight took d4: two pawns up. Better for him. [%csl Rd4] }) 10... Bf5 { His bishop guards the knight on e4. Hit that knight again: bring your queen knight to d2. [%cal Yb1d2,Rf5e4] } 11. Nd2 { That is the kings gambit: he is a pawn up, and with perfect play he may be a little better. But who plays perfectly? Your pieces are out, both kings still stand in the middle, and the e column is open: a board full of tactics. [%csl Re1,Re8] } (11. Nf2 { The knight hits e4 too, but it gets swapped off. [%cal Bf2e4] } 11... Nxf2 12. Kxf2 { The knights are gone, your king stands on f2, and he keeps his extra pawn with nothing to fear. Better for him. [%csl Rf2] }) (11. d5 { Kicking his knight hands it a better square. } 11... Ne5 12. Bxe5 dxe5 { Your push gave his knight e5, and after the trade he has both bishops and still the extra pawn. Better for him. [%csl Re5] }) 1-0


