{ Your knight stands on e5, and his extra pawn sits on f4, held up by his g pawn. }
5... d6 { His d pawn kicks your knight, and it has to move. One square wins back the gambit pawn. Which one? } 6. Nxg4 { The knight takes g4, and the gambit pawn is back: material is level. [%csl Gg4] } (6. d4 { Guarding the knight worked a move ago, but now his pawn attacks it. } 6... dxe5 { His pawn took your knight for nothing. Better for him. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Nd3 { Back to d3, hitting f4, but his bishop can guard that pawn. } 6... Bh6 { Better for him: his bishop guards f4, and you are still a pawn down. [%cal Rh6f4] }) (6. Nc4 { The knight steps back to c4, far from the action. } 6... Nf6 7. d4 Bh6 { Better for him: he keeps the pawn on f4 with his bishop guarding it, and your knight on c4 has no job. [%cal Rh6f4] }) (6. Nf3 { Straight back to f3, but his pawn is waiting there. } 6... gxf3 7. Qxf3 { Your queen took back on f3, but the knight is gone for a pawn. Better for him. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (6. Nxf7 { A knight for a pawn is too big a price. } 6... Kxf7 7. Bc4+ Ke8 { His king walked back home, and you are a piece down for one pawn. Better for him. [%csl Re8] }) (6. Qxg4 { Your queen grabs the pawn, but his bishop is waiting. } 6... Bxg4 { His bishop took your queen on g4. Lost for you. [%csl Rg4] }) (6. Bb5+ { A check first, but it wins nothing. } 6... c6 7. Nxg4 cxb5 { You got the g4 pawn back, but he took your bishop: a piece down. Better for him. [%csl Rb5] }) 6... Nf6 { His knight hits yours, and so does his light bishop from c8. His bishop to e7 was the other try, when your d pawn takes the middle. What do you do? [%cal Rc8g4] } 7. Nxf6+ { Swap it off with check: the knight that hit yours is gone. [%cal Gg4f6] } (7. Nf2 { Stepping back keeps the knight, but on f2 it does little. } 7... Rg8 8. d4 Bh6 { About level, but his rook and bishop came out with gain of time while your knight sits on f2. [%csl Rf2] }) (7. Bb5+ { A check first is a little slower. } 7... c6 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 9. Be2 { About level: after the trade your bishop had to step back, and he got c6 for free. [%csl Be2] }) (7. Be2 { The bishop guards the knight, but his knight grabs e4 instead. [%cal Be2g4] } 7... Nxe4 { His knight took your e pawn: he is a pawn up again. Slightly better for him. [%csl Re4] }) (7. d4 { The pawn takes the middle, but your knight is still hit twice. } 7... Bxg4 { His bishop took your knight, and if your queen takes back, his knight takes her. A piece down: better for him. [%csl Rg4] }) (7. Qf3 { Your queen guards the knight, but it is still hit twice. [%cal Bf3g4] } 7... Nxg4 8. d4 { His knight took yours, and your queen could not take back because of his bishop. A piece down: better for him. [%csl Rg4] }) (7. Qe2 { The queen guards the knight from e2, but two attackers beat one guard. [%cal Be2g4] } 7... Bxg4 { His bishop took your knight with a hit on your queen: a piece down. Better for him. [%cal Rg4e2] }) (7. e5 { Your pawn hits his knight, but his knight strikes first. } 7... Nxg4 { His knight simply took yours, and your pawn push hit nothing. Better for him. [%csl Rg4] }) 7... Qxf6 { His queen takes back, and material is level. Now she looks down the long diagonal at your queen side. Bring a piece out, but which one? [%cal Rf6b2] } 8. Nc3 { That is how the kings gambit wins its pawn back: material is level, and a pair of knights came off. Your knight blocks his queen's long diagonal and eyes d5. [%cal Gc3d5,Rf6c3] } (8. d4 { Just as good: the pawn blocks the diagonal and takes the middle. } 8... Nc6 { Level: your pawn blocks his queen's diagonal, and his knight hits it at once. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (8. Qf3 { The queen comes out to face his, a little slower. } 8... Nc6 9. Bb5 { Level: your queen came out early, and his knight joined the game with no trouble. [%csl Bf3] }) (8. Bb5+ { A bishop check, but his knight blocks and nothing is gained. } 8... Nd7 9. d4 Rg8 { Level, but his rook already hits your g pawn, and your bishop on b5 has no target. [%cal Rg8g2] }) (8. Bd3 { The bishop comes out, but it blocks your d pawn. } 8... Nc6 9. Nc3 Bh6 { Slightly better for him: his pieces come out fast, and your bishop on d3 blocks your d pawn. [%csl Rd3] }) (8. Be2 { A quiet move that leaves your g pawn weak. } 8... Rg8 9. Bf3 { Slightly better for him: your bishop is tied to guarding g2, and his pieces come out fast. [%cal Rg8g2,Gf3g2] }) (8. Bc4 { The bishop comes out, but it does not guard your g pawn. } 8... Rg8 9. d3 Rxg2 { His rook took your g pawn and sits in your camp: a pawn down. Better for him. [%csl Rg2] }) (8. Qe2 { The queen steps up, but she blocks your bishop. } 8... Nc6 9. d3 Nd4 { Slightly better for him: his knight lands on d4 and hits your queen. [%cal Rd4e2] }) 1-0


