{ Your knight stands on e5, deep in his half of the board. }
5... h5 { He pushes his h pawn to guard g4, and that is a mistake: his knight to f6 was the move. How do you make him pay for the lost time? [%cal Rh5g4] } 6. Bc4 { Your bishop joins your knight against f7, and only his king guards it. [%cal Gc4f7,Ge5f7] } (6. d4 { Taking the middle is fine, but a little slow here. } 6... d6 { Level: his d pawn kicks your knight, and with no bishop on c4 his f7 pawn is safe. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (6. Nc3 { Developing, but it leaves your knight on e5 without support. } 6... d6 7. Nxf7 Kxf7 { His d pawn kicked your knight, and the best it could do was give itself up on f7: a knight for a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (6. Bb5 { From b5 the bishop hits nothing. } 6... Be7 7. Bc4 Bxh4+ 8. Kf1 { His bishop took your h pawn with check, and your king had to walk. Two pawns down: better for him. [%cal Re7h4] }) (6. d3 { The pawn guards e4, but your knight on e5 gets chased. [%cal Bd3e4] } 6... d6 7. Nc4 Bh6 { Better for him: your knight was pushed back, his bishop guards f4, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%cal Rh6f4] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen steps up, but she blocks your own bishop. } 6... d6 7. Nc4 Nc6 { Better for him: your knight was chased back to c4, and your queen still blocks your light bishop. [%csl Re2] }) (6. Be2 { The bishop comes out on the quiet side, and f7 is left alone. } 6... d6 { Better for him: his d pawn kicks your knight, and it has no good square. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (6. Bd3 { Your bishop comes to d3, where it blocks your d pawn. } 6... Qe7 { Better for him: his queen hits your knight, and your d pawn cannot come to guard it. [%cal Re7e5] }) 6... Nh6 { His knight comes out to guard f7, and his rook to h7 was the other way to do it. Now your knight is the one without a guard. What next? [%cal Rh6f7] } 7. d4 { The pawn guards your knight and opens a road for your dark bishop to f4. [%cal Gd4e5,Gc1f4] } (7. Nc3 { Developing, but it lets his f pawn run forward. } 7... f3 8. Nxf3 gxf3 9. Qxf3 { Your knight took on f3 and his pawn took it back: a knight for two pawns. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rf3] }) (7. d3 { The pawn guards e4, but it leaves your knight without support. [%cal Bd3e4] } 7... d6 8. Nxf7 Nxf7 { With nowhere good to go after d6, your knight gave itself up on f7: a knight for a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (7. c3 { The pawn prepares d4 a move too late, and his f pawn runs first. } 7... f3 8. gxf3 d6 { Better for him: material is level, but his d pawn hits your knight, and your king side pawns are split up. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (7. Nxf7 { Grabbing f7 now costs the knight. } 7... Nxf7 { His knight took yours on f7: a knight for a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (7. Bxf7+ { The bishop grabs f7 with check, but the sums do not work. } 7... Nxf7 8. Nxf7 Kxf7 { You took a pawn and a knight, but gave your bishop and knight: a piece for a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (7. O-O { Castling first leaves your knight on e5 all alone. } 7... Nc6 8. Nxc6 dxc6 { Better for him: the knights came off, and he keeps the extra pawn with a free game. [%csl Rf4] }) (7. Qe2 { Your queen guards the knight, but she blocks your bishop. [%cal Be2e5] } 7... f3 8. Qf2 d6 { Better for him: his f pawn drove your queen away, and now his d pawn kicks your knight. [%cal Rd6e5] }) 7... d6 { His d pawn kicks your knight again. Where does it go? } 8. Nd3 { From d3 the knight hits f4, the pawn he took on move two. Against a bishop move, your bishop would take that pawn. [%cal Gd3f4] } (8. Nxf7 { Grabbing f7 costs your knight. } 8... Nxf7 9. Bxf4 Qe7 { You have two pawns for the knight, and his king is safe enough. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (8. Bxf7+ { A check on f7, but only a pawn to show for it. } 8... Nxf7 9. Nxf7 Kxf7 { Your bishop and knight are both gone for his knight and a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rf7] }) (8. Bxf4 { Taking the pawn first leaves your knight to his d pawn. } 8... dxe5 9. Bxe5 f6 { His d pawn took your knight, and his f pawn now chases your bishop. Two pawns for a piece: better for him. [%csl Re5] }) (8. Nf3 { Back to f3, where his pawn simply takes it. } 8... gxf3 9. gxf3 { His pawn took your knight, and your g pawn took back only a pawn. Better for him. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (8. Nxg4 { The knight grabs g4, but it is not safe there. } 8... Nxg4 { His knight took yours: a piece for a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rg4] }) 8... f3 { He pushes the pawn on to f3, where it hits your g pawn. Against a queen move, your knight would come to c3. Take it or leave it? } 9. gxf3 { Material is level: the gambit pawn is back. If he takes on f3, your queen takes back, and a pawn run to g3 would leave it alone deep in your camp. [%csl Gf3] } (9. g3 { Keeping the pawns closed lets his pawn on f3 stay. } 9... d5 10. Bxd5 c6 { About level: material is even after your bishop took on d5, but his pawn on f3 is a thorn in your camp. [%csl Rf3] }) (9. Nf4 { The knight comes back to f4, but his pawn on f3 stays. } 9... Be7 10. Nc3 Bxh4+ { About level, though he is two pawns up: his pawn on f3 still stands, and his bishop took h4 with check. [%cal Re7h4] }) (9. Kf1 { The king steps aside, but it cannot castle any more. } 9... Nc6 { About level: his pawn on f3 stays, and your king has lost the right to castle. [%csl Rf1] }) (9. Be3 { Developing, but his pawn takes on g2. } 9... fxg2 10. Rg1 Qxh4+ { His pawn took on g2 and his queen took h4 with check: three pawns up. Better for him. [%cal Rd8h4] }) (9. Qxf3 { The queen takes, but his g pawn guards f3. [%cal Rg4f3] } 9... gxf3 { His g pawn took your queen: lost for you. [%csl Rf3] }) (9. O-O { Castling now puts your king right next to his pawn on f3. } 9... Qxh4 10. Nd2 Bg7 { Better for him: his queen took your h pawn, and his pawn still sits next to your king. [%csl Rf3] }) (9. Nc3 { Developing, but his pawn takes on g2 first. } 9... fxg2 10. Rg1 Qxh4+ { His queen checks from h4 after his pawn took on g2: three pawns up now. Better for him. [%cal Rd8h4] }) 9... Be7 { His bishop aims at your pawn on h4. Does that pawn need saving? [%cal Re7h4] } 10. Be3 { Let the pawn go: your bishop comes out and guards d4, and your king gets a road to d2. [%cal Ge3d4] } (10. Bf4 { Just as good: the bishop comes out with an eye on d6. [%cal Gf4d6] } 10... Nc6 { Level: your bishop is out, and his bishop still eyes your h pawn. [%cal Re7h4] }) (10. fxg4 { Taking g4 opens lines for his pieces instead of yours. } 10... Bxh4+ 11. Kf1 Nc6 { Better for him: his bishop took your h pawn with check, your king cannot castle, and his knight eyes g4. [%cal Rh6g4] }) (10. Nc3 { Developing is fine, but a little slower. } 10... Bxh4+ 11. Kf1 { About level: his bishop took h4 with check, and your king had to walk. [%cal Rh4e1] }) (10. Bg5 { The bishop blocks the diagonal, but he trades it off. } 10... Nc6 11. c3 Bxg5 12. hxg5 { Level for now: your pawn on g5 stands alone, and his queen attacks it. [%cal Rd8g5] }) (10. Bxh6 { Taking the knight gives up your good bishop. } 10... Rxh6 11. Kd2 Nc6 { Slightly better for him: bishop for knight, and his bishop on e7 now has no rival on the dark squares. [%csl Re7] }) (10. Qe2 { The queen steps up, but she walks into his plans. } 10... Bxh4+ 11. Kf1 gxf3 { Better for him: he took h4 with check and then f3 with a hit on your queen. [%cal Rf3e2] }) (10. O-O { Castling puts your king on the side where his pieces are aiming. } 10... Nc6 11. c3 Bxh4 { Better for him: his bishop took your h pawn, and his pieces face your castled king. [%csl Rg1] }) 10... Bxh4+ { His bishop takes the pawn with check. Where does your king go? } 11. Kd2 { On d2 the king steps off his bishop's diagonal and stands behind your centre pawns. Had he taken on f3 instead, your queen would take back. [%csl Gd2] } (11. Kf1 { The king steps aside, but his pawn takes on f3 and opens more lines. } 11... gxf3 12. Nc3 Nc6 { About level, but he is two pawns up, and your king cannot castle. [%csl Rf1] }) (11. Ke2 { Your king steps forward to e2, right into his pawn's check. } 11... gxf3+ 12. Kd2 Bg4 { Better for him: his pawn took f3 with check, and your king still had to go to d2. Two pawns down. [%csl Rd2] }) (11. Nf2 { The knight blocks the check, but his pawn moves up to hit it. } 11... g3 12. Qd2 gxf2+ { His pawn took your knight with check. Better for him. [%csl Rf2] }) (11. Rxh4 { The rook takes, but his queen takes it back with check. } 11... Qxh4+ { His queen took your rook with check: a rook for a bishop. Better for him. [%csl Rh4] }) 11... Nc6 { His knight comes out and eyes d4, which your bishop guards. What is your move? [%cal Rc6d4,Ge3d4] } 12. Nc3 { A pawn down, but four of your pieces are out against three of his, and your king is safe on d2. Level: that is the kings gambit trade, a pawn for time. [%cal Gc3d5] } (12. Qg1 { Just as good: your queen comes to the g column. } 12... Kf8 { Slightly better for you: your queen on g1 eyes his king side, and his king stepped to f8. [%csl Gg1] }) (12. Bb5 { Pinning the knight is a little slower. } 12... gxf3 13. Qxf3 Ng4 { About level: the pawns came off on f3, and his knight jumped to g4. [%csl Rg4] }) (12. c3 { Guarding d4 with a pawn is a little slow. [%cal Bc3d4] } 12... gxf3 13. Qxf3 Bg4 { About level: his bishop comes out with a hit on your queen. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (12. Bd5 { The bishop hits his knight, but it just gets swapped. } 12... gxf3 13. Bxc6+ bxc6 { About level: your good bishop is gone, and his pawn on f3 still stands. [%csl Rf3] }) (12. d5 { Kicking his knight sends it to a better square. } 12... Ne5 13. Nxe5 dxe5 { Slightly better for him: the knights came off, and your pawn push left holes in your centre. [%csl Re5] }) (12. e5 { Pushing past gives his pieces new squares. } 12... dxe5 13. Bb5 Nf5 { Better for him: your centre is gone, and his knight jumps in with gain of time. [%csl Rf5] }) (12. Qe2 { The queen steps up into his pawn's reach. } 12... gxf3 13. Qxf3 Bg4 { Better for him: his bishop hits your queen, and she has to move again. [%cal Rg4f3] }) 1-0


