{ Gambit accepted: his extra pawn sits on f4, and your knight guards h4. [%cal Gf3h4] }
3... d5 { He hits your king pawn with his d pawn, ready to hand a pawn back to free his pieces. What now? } 4. exd5 { Take it: material is level again. Had his queen taken back on d5, your knight would come to c3 and hit her. [%csl Gd5] } (4. e5 { Pushing past keeps lines closed, and his pawn on d5 stays strong. } 4... g5 5. h3 Ne7 { Better for him: he keeps the extra pawn and a big pawn in the middle, and your e pawn is cut off. [%csl Rd5] }) (4. Nc3 { The knight guards your e pawn, but his d pawn simply steps past. [%cal Bc3e4] } 4... d4 5. Ne2 g5 { Slightly better for him: his pawn on d4 cramps you, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (4. d3 { Solid, but it leaves him a pawn up. [%cal Bd3e4] } 4... g5 { Slightly better for him: his pawns hold f4, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rf4] }) (4. Qe2 { Your queen covers the e pawn, but she stands in front of your bishop. [%cal Be2e4] } 4... Nf6 5. d3 Be7 { Slightly better for him: he keeps the extra pawn, and your queen is in the way. [%csl Re2] }) (4. Bd3 { The bishop guards e4, but his pawn takes anyway. [%cal Bd3e4] } 4... dxe4 5. Bxe4 Bd6 { Slightly better for him: the pawns came off, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rf4] }) (4. Bb5+ { A check first, but your e pawn is still hit. } 4... c6 5. Bd3 dxe4 { Better for him: he took your e pawn and is two pawns up. [%csl Re4] }) (4. d4 { Taking the middle leaves your e pawn to his d pawn. } 4... dxe4 5. Qe2 Qe7 { Better for him: the e pawn is gone, and his queen guards the pawn that took it. [%csl Re4] }) 4... Nf6 { His knight hits your pawn on d5. How do you meet it? [%cal Rf6d5] } 5. Bb5+ { A check first: he has to answer it before he can take on d5. Against a bishop move to block, you simply take that bishop. [%cal Gb5e8] } (5. c4 { Just as good: your c pawn guards d5. [%cal Bc4d5] } 5... c6 { Level: he hits d5 once more, and your c pawn holds it. [%cal Bc4d5] }) (5. Bc4 { Just as good: the bishop guards d5 and looks at f7. [%cal Bc4d5] } 5... Nxd5 { About level: he won the pawn back, and your bishop still aims at f7. [%cal Gc4f7] }) (5. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight guards d5. [%cal Bc3d5] } 5... Nxd5 6. Bc4 Nxc3 7. bxc3 { About level: the knights came off, and your bishop eyes f7. [%cal Gc4f7] }) (5. d4 { Just as good: the middle pawn comes first. } 5... Nxd5 6. Bc4 Be6 { About level: he won the d5 pawn back, and his bishop and knight guard each other. [%csl Rd5] }) (5. Be2 { Nearly as good: a quiet bishop move. } 5... Nxd5 { About level: he won the pawn back, and he keeps the extra one on f4. [%csl Rf4] }) (5. Qe2+ { The queen check blocks your own bishop. } 5... Be7 6. Nc3 O-O { Better for him: his king is safe, and your queen stands in front of your bishop. [%csl Re2] }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop comes out, but it does nothing for d5. } 5... Nxd5 { Slightly better for him: he won the pawn back, and your bishop blocks your d pawn. [%csl Rd3] }) 5... c6 { He blocks with his c pawn and offers it. Your bishop is attacked. What now? } 6. dxc6 { Take it: your pawn grabs his instead of being grabbed. Had the b pawn taken back, your bishop would step back to c4 and aim at f7. [%cal Gd5c6] } (6. Ba4 { The bishop steps back but lets his knight take d5. } 6... Nxd5 7. c4 Nb6 { About level: he won the pawn back and chases your bishop again. [%csl Ra4] }) (6. Qe2+ { The queen check blocks his bishop, but also your king. } 6... Be7 7. dxc6 Nxc6 { Slightly better for him: material is level, but your queen blocks your own bishop's retreat. [%csl Re2] }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop steps back, and the pawn on d5 falls. } 6... cxd5 { Better for him: his pawn took d5, and he is a pawn up with a strong middle. [%csl Rd5] }) (6. Be2 { Back to e2, and his pawn takes d5. } 6... cxd5 { Better for him: your extra pawn is gone, so he keeps the gambit pawn. [%csl Rd5] }) (6. Bd3 { The bishop steps back, but d5 still falls. } 6... cxd5 7. Nc3 { Better for him: his pawn took d5, and your bishop on d3 blocks your d pawn. [%csl Rd3] }) (6. Bxc6+ { Giving the bishop for a pawn is far too much. } 6... bxc6 7. O-O cxd5 { He took the bishop and then your d pawn. A piece down: better for him. [%csl Rd5] }) (6. O-O { Castling ignores your bishop, and his pawn takes it. } 6... cxb5 { His pawn took your bishop: a piece for a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rb5] }) 6... Nxc6 { His knight takes back, and material is level. Watch his queen before you tuck your king away. } 7. d4 { The pawn takes the middle and shuts the diagonal from b6 to g1, where your king wants to go. [%cal Gd4b6] } (7. Nc3 { A touch better, in fact: the knight joins the game first. } 7... Be6 { About level: your pieces come out, and your bishop pins his knight. [%cal Gb5e8] }) (7. c3 { Just as good: the c pawn gets ready for d4. } 7... Bd6 { About level: your pawn prepares the middle, and his bishop comes out. [%csl Gc3] }) (7. O-O { Castling now walks into a queen check. } 7... Qb6+ 8. d4 Qxb5 { His queen checked from b6 and then took your bishop: a piece down. Better for him. [%cal Rb6b5] }) (7. Ne5 { The knight jumps forward, but his queen hits it and your bishop. } 7... Qb6 8. Qe2 Be6 { Slightly better for him: his queen and bishop are active, and your pieces are tied up. [%csl Re5] }) (7. Bxc6+ { Bishop for knight is just as good, but it hands him two bishops. } 7... bxc6 8. Nc3 Be7 { About level: your bishop is gone, and his bishops have open lines. [%csl Bc6] }) (7. Qe2+ { A check that only blocks your own bishop. } 7... Be7 { Slightly better for him: your queen stands in the way of your bishop. [%csl Re2] }) 7... Bd6 { His bishop comes out. Had his queen checked from a5, your knight would block on c3. What now? } 8. O-O { Castled and safe: your pawn on d4 shuts his queen's check, and your kings gambit pawn is back. With your queen knight and dark bishop at home, he may be a little better with perfect play. But his king still stands in the middle. [%cal Gb5e8][%csl Gd4] } (8. c3 { Just as good: the pawn guards d4. [%cal Bc3d4] } 8... h6 { About level: your pawn chain holds the middle, and his king waits to castle. [%csl Gd4] }) (8. Nbd2 { Just as good: the queen knight heads for c4. } 8... O-O 9. O-O { About level: both kings are safe, and your knight is on its way to c4. [%cal Gd2c4] }) (8. c4 { Just as good: the pawn gains room on the queen side. } 8... O-O { About level: his king is safe, and your pawns on c4 and d4 stand side by side. [%csl Gc4] }) (8. Qe2+ { The check only blocks your own pieces. } 8... Be6 9. Bc4 O-O { About level: his king got to safety, and your queen and bishop were pushed around. [%csl Re2] }) (8. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but it blocks your c pawn. } 8... O-O 9. O-O h6 { Slightly better for him: both kings are safe, and your c pawn can no longer help d4. [%csl Rc3] }) (8. Bd3 { Nearly as good: the bishop drops back, though it gives up the pin. } 8... Qe7+ 9. Qe2 Qxe2+ 10. Bxe2 { About level: the queens came off, and your bishop gave up its pin on his knight. [%csl Be2] }) (8. Bxf4 { Grabbing the gambit pawn loses your bishop. } 8... Bxf4 { His bishop took yours, and nothing can take back. A piece for a pawn: better for him. [%csl Rf4] }) 1-0


