3... Nf6 { His knight comes out and hits your king pawn. How do you answer? [%cal Rf6e4] } 4. e5 { The pawn kicks his knight away from the middle. [%cal Ge5f6] } (4. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight guards e4. [%cal Bc3e4] } 4... d5 { About level: he strikes in the middle at once, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rf4] }) (4. d4 { Just as good: take the middle and let e4 go. } 4... Nxe4 5. Bd3 d5 { About level: he took e4, but your bishop hits his knight and you can castle. [%cal Gd3e4] }) (4. d3 { Just as good: the pawn guards e4. [%cal Bd3e4] } 4... d5 5. Bxf4 Nc6 { About level: you took the gambit pawn back, and his pieces are out. [%csl Gf4] }) (4. Bc4 { Nearly as good: the bishop comes out and lets his knight take e4. } 4... Nxe4 5. Qe2 Qe7 { About level, but he is a pawn up and ready to trade queens. [%csl Re4] }) (4. Qe2 { Nearly as good: your queen shields e4, though she hems in your light bishop. [%cal Be2e4] } 4... d5 5. d3 Be7 { About level: he keeps the extra pawn, and your queen is in the way. [%csl Re2] }) (4. Be2 { A quiet bishop move that lets his knight take e4. } 4... Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: he took your e pawn and is two pawns up. [%csl Re4] }) 4... Nh5 { His knight goes to the edge, guarding the pawn on f4. Had it jumped to d5, your queen knight would meet it. Your move. [%cal Rh5f4] } 5. d4 { The pawn takes the middle and guards e5. [%cal Gd4e5] } (5. Qe2 { Just as good: the queen guards e5 first. [%cal Be2e5] } 5... Be7 { About level: your queen guards e5, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Ge5] }) (5. Be2 { Just as good: the bishop comes out and you can castle. } 5... d6 6. O-O g5 { About level: your king is safe, and his pawns hold f4. [%csl Gg1] }) (5. Nc3 { A little slower: your queen knight joins in, and he strikes in the middle first. } 5... d5 { About level: he strikes in the middle before you can push d4. [%csl Rd5] }) (5. Bc4 { The bishop comes out, but he hits it with d5. } 5... d5 6. exd6 Bxd6 { Slightly better for him: you had to take on d6, and his bishop came out with gain of time. [%csl Rd6] }) (5. Bb5 { Nearly as good, though from b5 the bishop does little. } 5... g6 { About level: he keeps the extra pawn, and his bishop gets ready to come to g7. [%csl Rf4] }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop comes out, but it does not guard e5. [%csl Re5] } 5... d6 6. O-O dxe5 { Better for him: he took your e pawn and keeps the extra one on f4. [%csl Re5] }) (5. d3 { The small pawn step leaves e5 alone. } 5... d6 { Slightly better for him: his d pawn hits e5, and your pawns are stuck. [%cal Rd6e5] }) 5... d6 { He hits your pawn on e5. Against d5, your king bishop comes out and you castle. What now? [%cal Rd6e5] } 6. Qe2 { Your queen guards e5 along the e column, and she looks at his king behind it. Against a capture on e5, your knight takes back. [%cal Ge2e5,Ge5e8] } (6. Nc3 { Nearly as good: your knight develops and watches d5. [%cal Bc3d5] } 6... dxe5 { About level: he took your e pawn, and your d pawn stands alone in the middle. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop comes out, a little slow here. } 6... Nc6 7. O-O g6 { About level: his knight hits your middle, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (6. exd6 { Taking on d6 frees his bishop. } 6... Bxd6 7. Qe2+ Qe7 { About level: his bishop came out with gain of time, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rd6] }) (6. Bb5+ { The bishop check is met by a pawn that hits it. } 6... c6 7. Bc4 d5 { About level: his pawns hit your bishop twice, and it had to move again. [%csl Rd5] }) (6. Bxf4 { Grabbing the pawn on f4 drops your bishop. } 6... Nxf4 { His knight on h5 took your bishop. Better for him. [%csl Rf4] }) (6. Bd2 { The bishop steps to d2 and leaves e5 without help. } 6... dxe5 7. dxe5 g5 { Better for him: he keeps the extra pawn, and your e5 pawn is weak. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Be2 { A quiet move, but e5 is left alone. } 6... dxe5 7. O-O exd4 { Slightly better for him: he took two pawns in the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. Bd3 { Your bishop comes to d3, but nothing new guards e5. [%csl Re5] } 6... dxe5 7. O-O g6 { Slightly better for him: he took e5, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Re5] }) 6... Be7 { He develops his bishop, and that is a mistake: d5 was the move. Can you punish it? } 7. exd6 { Your pawn takes on d6, and his bishop on e7 is pinned to his king: it cannot take back. [%cal Ge2e8] } (7. Nc3 { Developing is natural, but his bishop gets out. } 7... Bg4 8. Bd2 dxe5 9. dxe5 { Slightly better for him: the chance is gone, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rf4] }) (7. Bd2 { Developing, but the chance slips away. } 7... Bg4 { Slightly better for him: his bishop pins your knight, and the moment is gone. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (7. g3 { Attacking f4 is too slow. } 7... Bg4 8. Qg2 Nc6 { Better for him: his bishop pins your knight, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (7. Qe4 { The queen hits nothing new on e4. } 7... d5 8. Qe2 c5 { Better for him: his pawns chased your queen, and his middle is strong. [%csl Rd5] }) (7. Bxf4 { Taking on f4 now drops your bishop to his knight. } 7... Nxf4 { His knight took your bishop, and the chance to punish him is gone. Better for him. [%csl Rf4] }) 7... Qxd6 { His queen takes back. Is anything of his left loose? } 8. Qb5+ { Check, and your queen hits his knight on h5 too: one move, two targets. [%cal Gb5e8,Gb5h5] } (8. Nc3 { Developing, but it misses the check that hits two things. } 8... O-O 9. Bd2 Bf5 { About level: his king castled, and the chance to win the knight is gone. [%csl Gh5] }) (8. Bd2 { The bishop comes out, and the fork slips away. } 8... O-O { Slightly better for him: he castled, so your queen can no longer check him on b5. [%csl Rb5] }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps forward, but it wins nothing. } 8... g6 9. Qf2 c5 { Slightly better for him: his pawn hits your centre, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Na3 { Your knight heads for the edge, a slow plan. } 8... Nc6 9. Bd2 O-O { Slightly better for him: his king is safe, and your knight on a3 does little. [%csl Ra3] }) (8. Bxf4 { Grabbing f4 hands your bishop to his knight. } 8... Nxf4 { His knight took your bishop instead of losing itself. Better for him. [%csl Rf4] }) 8... Nc6 { He blocks the check with his knight. Now collect your prize. } 9. Qxh5 { Your queen takes the knight on h5: a whole piece won. [%csl Gh5] } (9. Bd2 { Developing, but the knight on h5 gets away. } 9... Nf6 { Better for him: his knight escaped to f6, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rf6] }) (9. Nbd2 { Developing, but you forgot the knight on h5. } 9... g6 10. c3 a6 { Better for him: his knight is guarded now, and your queen is chased. [%csl Rh5] }) (9. Be2 { Developing, but the knight on h5 is still his. } 9... Bg4 10. O-O O-O-O { Better for him: his knight on h5 is safe, and his pieces aim at your centre. [%csl Rd4] }) (9. Na3 { Guarding against checks, but the knight on h5 escapes. } 9... g6 10. Nc4 Qb4+ { Better for him: his knight is guarded, and his queen checks. [%csl Rb4] }) (9. d5 { Kicking his knight shuts your queen off from h5. } 9... a6 { Better for him: your queen is hit, and your own pawn now blocks her road to his knight. [%cal Ra6b5] }) (9. Nc3 { Developing, and the knight on h5 gets time to be guarded. } 9... Bg4 { Better for him: his bishop comes out and now guards the knight on h5. [%cal Rg4h5] }) 9... Nxd4 { His knight grabs your d pawn and threatens a fork on c2. What do you do? [%csl Rc2] } 10. Nxd4 { Your knight takes his knight before it can fork you. [%csl Gd4] } (10. Bd3 { The bishop guards c2, but his knight takes on f3 with check. [%cal Bd3c2] } 10... g6 11. Qa5 Nxf3+ 12. gxf3 { Level: you keep a knight for two pawns, but your king side pawns are broken. [%csl Rf3] }) (10. Bb5+ { A check first, but his knight takes your bishop. } 10... Nxb5 11. Qxb5+ Bd7 { Level: bishop and knight were swapped, and your advantage has melted away. [%csl Rb5] }) (10. Na3 { The knight guards c2, but it is slow. [%cal Ba3c2] } 10... g6 11. Bb5+ c6 { Better for him: your queen and bishop are both under attack. [%csl Rb5] }) (10. Bd2 { Developing, but it allows the fork. } 10... Nxc2+ 11. Kd1 Bf5 { His knight checked from c2 and hits your rook in the corner. Better for him. [%cal Rc2a1] }) (10. c3 { Kicking his knight is natural, but it jumps in first. } 10... Nc2+ { His knight jumped to c2 with check and forks your king and rook. Better for him. [%cal Rc2e1,Rc2a1] }) 10... Qxd4 { His queen takes back and sits in the middle. Your move. } 11. Nc3 { Your gambit pawn is repaid with interest: a knight for two pawns, better for you. Your last knight eyes b5, where it would hit his queen and c7. [%cal Gc3b5] } (11. Bb5+ { The check is a little less strong. } 11... c6 12. Bd3 g6 { Still better for you, with the extra piece, but your bishop had to step back. [%csl Gd3] }) (11. Bd2 { Developing, but his queen can grab your b pawn. } 11... g6 12. Qe2 Qxb2 { Still better for you, but his queen took your b pawn. [%csl Rb2] }) (11. Be2 { A quiet bishop move. } 11... Be6 { Still better for you with the extra piece, but he develops fast. [%csl Ge2] }) (11. Nd2 { The knight comes out, but it blocks your bishop. } 11... O-O 12. Bd3 g6 { Still better for you, but your knight on d2 blocks your dark bishop. [%csl Rd2] }) (11. Bd3 { The bishop comes out, a little slow. } 11... Bd7 12. Nc3 O-O-O { Your extra piece keeps it better for you, but his king castled to safety. [%csl Gd3] }) (11. c3 { Kicking his queen gives back much of your edge. } 11... Qe4+ { Level: his queen checks from e4, and your development is behind. [%csl Re1] }) (11. Bxf4 { Grabbing f4 hangs your bishop to his queen. } 11... Qxf4 { His queen took your bishop, and he is ahead in material. Better for him. [%csl Rf4] }) 1-0


