{ The kings gambit: your f pawn stands on f4, offered to his e pawn. }
2... d5 { He turns the gambit down and hits back in the middle with his d pawn. What do you do? } 3. exd5 { Take it, and you are a pawn up. Had he offered a second pawn with c6, your knight would come to c3. [%csl Gd5] } (3. Nf3 { Just as good: the king knight comes out first. } 3... dxe4 { Level: the middle pawns trade places, and the game stays calm. [%csl Be4] }) (3. Nc3 { The knight guards e4, but his d pawn pushes on to d4. [%cal Bc3e4] } 3... d4 4. Nce2 Nc6 { About level: his pawn on d4 cramps your pieces. [%csl Rd4] }) (3. d3 { The pawn guards e4, but his pawn takes anyway. [%cal Bd3e4] } 3... dxe4 4. Nc3 Nc6 { About level: the middle opened, and his pieces come out fast. [%csl Rc6] }) (3. d4 { Two pawns in the middle look strong, but his pawn takes on d4. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nf6 { About level: your queen took back, and his knight comes out with gain of time on her. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (3. fxe5 { Grabbing the pawn opens a road for his queen. } 3... Qh4+ 4. g3 Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 { His queen checked twice and now hits your rook in the corner. Better for him. [%cal Re4h1] }) (3. Qe2 { Your queen steps up to guard e4 and blocks your bishop. [%cal Be2e4] } 3... dxe4 { Slightly better for him: he took your e pawn, and your queen stands in front of your bishop. [%csl Re2] }) (3. Bb5+ { A check first, but it lets him block and hit back. } 3... c6 4. Be2 exf4 { Better for him: your bishop went back, and he took the f pawn after all. [%csl Rf4] }) 3... e4 { He pushes past to cramp you, and that is a mistake: taking on f4 was better, when your knight comes to f3. What now? } 4. d3 { The pawn hits his pawn on e4, and your extra pawn on d5 stays. [%cal Gd3e4] } (4. Nc3 { Developing, but it leaves his pawn on e4 alone. } 4... Nf6 { Level: his knight hits your pawn on d5, and his pawn still cramps you. [%cal Rf6d5] }) (4. d4 { The pawn takes the middle but lets his pawn stay on e4. } 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 Nxd5 { Level: he won the pawn back, and his pawn on e4 is safe. [%csl Re4] }) (4. Bc4 { The bishop guards d5, but his pawn on e4 stays. [%cal Bc4d5] } 4... c6 { Level: his c pawn hits d5, which has only your bishop to guard it. [%cal Rc6d5] }) (4. Bb5+ { A check first, but it lets him hit back at d5. } 4... c6 5. dxc6 Nxc6 { Level: the pawn went back to him, and his pieces are out. [%csl Rc6] }) (4. Qe2 { Your queen attacks e4, but she blocks your king bishop. [%cal Be2e4] } 4... Be7 5. Nc3 Nf6 { Level: his pawn on e4 is guarded, and your extra pawn on d5 is under fire. [%csl Re4] }) (4. Bd3 { The bishop hits his pawn, but the pawn takes it. } 4... exd3 { His pawn took your bishop. Better for him. [%csl Rd3] }) (4. Be2 { A quiet bishop move, and his pawn on e4 stays. } 4... Nf6 { About level: his pawn on e4 stays, and your bishop on e2 hits nothing. [%csl Re4] }) (4. c4 { The pawn guards d5, but his pawn on e4 cramps you. [%cal Bc4d5] } 4... Nf6 5. d4 Bb4+ { About level: his bishop checks, and his pawn on e4 stays strong. [%csl Re4] }) (4. Nf3 { The knight comes out, but his pawn takes it. } 4... exf3 5. Qxf3 Nf6 { His pawn took your knight, and your queen got only a pawn back. Better for him. [%csl Rf3] }) 4... Nf6 { His knight comes out to guard his pawn. Had his queen taken on d5 instead, your knight would come to c3 and hit her. } 5. dxe4 { Take the pawn: now you are two pawns up. [%csl Ge4] } (5. Nc3 { Developing, but his pawn on e4 stays. } 5... Bb4 6. Bd2 exd3 { Level: he took on d3, and your extra pawn is gone. [%csl Rd3] }) (5. Qe2 { The queen hits e4, but his bishop comes out with a hit on her. } 5... Bg4 { Level: his bishop hits your queen, and your extra pawn on d5 is loose. [%cal Rg4e2] }) (5. Nd2 { The knight hits e4, but it blocks your bishop. } 5... exd3 6. Bxd3 Nxd5 { About level: he took d5 back, and the middle is open. [%csl Rd5] }) (5. c4 { The pawn guards d5, but e4 stays his. [%cal Bc4d5] } 5... c6 6. dxe4 Nxe4 { About level: he gave a pawn on c6 to open lines, and his pieces are active. [%csl Re4] }) (5. Be3 { Developing, but his knight takes d5. } 5... Nxd5 { Slightly better for him: he won the d5 pawn back, and his pawn on e4 still stands. [%csl Rd5] }) (5. Nf3 { Your knight comes out to f3, right where his pawn can take it. } 5... exf3 6. Qxf3 Bc5 { His pawn took your knight: a piece for a pawn. Better for him. [%csl Rf3] }) (5. Be2 { The bishop comes out, but e4 stays his. } 5... Qxd5 { About level: his queen took d5, and his pawn still stands on e4. [%csl Rd5] }) 5... Nxe4 { His knight takes back and stands in the middle. What now? } 6. Nf3 { The knight comes out and covers h4, so his queen has no check there. [%cal Gf3h4] } (6. Qe2 { Nearly as good: the queen hits his knight at once. [%cal Ge2e4] } 6... Qxd5 7. Nd2 f5 { Level: his queen took d5, and his knight is safe. [%csl Rd5] }) (6. Qf3 { The queen hits his knight and guards f4. [%cal Bf3e4,Bf3f4] } 6... Qxd5 7. Bd3 Qa5+ { Level: his queen took d5 and checks you. [%csl Rd5] }) (6. Be3 { Developing, but his queen checks on h4. } 6... Qh4+ 7. g3 Nxg3 { Level: his knight took your g pawn, and if you take it back, his queen takes your rook. [%csl Rg3] }) (6. Bb5+ { A check, but his pawn blocks and hits your bishop. } 6... c6 7. Qf3 cxb5 { About level: he took your bishop, and you have two pawns for it. [%csl Rb5] }) (6. Nc3 { The knight hits his knight, but h4 is open to his queen. } 6... Qh4+ 7. g3 Nxg3 { About level: his knight took your g pawn with a hit on your rook. [%cal Rg3h1] }) (6. Bd3 { Developing, but h4 is still open. } 6... Qh4+ 7. g3 Nxg3 { Better for him: his knight took your g pawn, and it cannot be taken back, because his queen would take your rook. [%csl Rg3] }) (6. Nd2 { The knight hits his knight, but h4 is open. } 6... Qh4+ 7. g3 Nxg3 8. hxg3 Qxh1 { Better for him: you took his knight, but his queen took your rook in the corner. [%csl Rh1] }) 6... Bc5 { His bishop comes out and aims at your king side. What now? } 7. Qe2 { Your queen pins his knight to his king. Against a bishop move with check, your king steps to d1; against a queen move or f5, your dark bishop comes out. [%cal Ge2e8] } (7. Nbd2 { Challenging the knight is a little slower. } 7... Nf2 8. Qe2+ Qe7 { Level: his knight hopped to f2 and hits your rook, and the queens face off. [%cal Rf2h1] }) (7. Bd3 { Developing, but his knight jumps to f2. } 7... Nf2 { Level: his knight on f2 hits your queen and your rook. [%cal Rf2h1,Rf2d1] }) (7. Bb5+ { A bishop check, but his pawn blocks and your bishop must go back. } 7... c6 8. Bd3 Nf2 { About level: his knight on f2 hits your rook and your queen. [%cal Rf2h1] }) (7. Qd3 { The queen hits his knight, but it is not pinned there. } 7... Qe7 8. Be3 Nd6 { About level: his knight stepped back, and he fights for d5. [%csl Rd6] }) (7. Nc3 { Developing, but his knight takes it. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 O-O { Better for him: your pawns are broken and your king stands in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) (7. Be2 { Developing, but you cannot castle. } 7... O-O 8. Rf1 Re8 { Better for him: his rook lines up against your king on the e column. [%cal Re8e1] }) (7. Bc4 { The bishop comes out, but your king stays in the middle. } 7... O-O 8. Rf1 Re8 { Better for him: his king is castled, and his rook stares down the open e column at your king. [%cal Re8e1] }) (7. Be3 { Developing, but he swaps and your king is stuck. } 7... Bxe3 8. Bb5+ c6 { Better for him: your dark bishop is gone, and your king cannot castle. [%csl Re1] }) 7... Bf5 { His bishop guards the knight. Grabbing d5 with his queen would have left that knight in trouble. What now? } 8. Nc3 { He gave a pawn to push past, and he never got it back: you are a pawn up on d5, and his knight on e4 is pinned and hit twice. [%cal Gc3e4,Ge2e4] } (8. Nbd2 { Nearly as good: this knight hits e4 too. [%cal Bd2e4] } 8... Qxd5 9. g4 Bf2+ { Slightly better for you, but less than before: his queen took d5, and his bishop checks. [%csl Rd5] }) (8. g4 { The pawn hits his bishop, but his king runs to safety. } 8... O-O 9. gxf5 Re8 { About level: you won his bishop, but your king is stuck on the e column facing his rook. [%cal Re8e1] }) (8. Be3 { Developing, but he trades and gets free. } 8... O-O 9. Bxc5 Nxc5 { About level: his knight escaped the pin, and his king is safe. [%csl Rc5] }) (8. Ng5 { The knight hits his knight, but he simply castles. } 8... O-O 9. Nxe4 Bxe4 { Better for him: the knights came off, and his bishop is strong in the middle. [%csl Re4] }) (8. Qb5+ { A check, but it gives up the pin. } 8... Nd7 9. Bd3 O-O { Better for him: his king is safe, and your queen is out of play. [%csl Rb5] }) (8. Nd4 { The knight hits his bishop, but his bishop takes it. } 8... Bxd4 { His bishop took your knight: a piece gone. Better for him. [%csl Rd4] }) 1-0


