{ Now learn the pawn centre: two of your pawns side by side in the middle of the board. He has taken your f pawn, and your knight stands on f3. }
3... d6 { He plays d6 to guard e5. Build your pawn centre: push your d pawn two squares. [%cal Yd2d4,Rd6e5] } 4. d4 { That is the pawn centre: your pawns on d4 and e4 stand side by side and own the middle. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (4. Bc4 { Just as good: the bishop aims at f7. } 4... h6 { About level: you can still build the pawn centre next move. [%cal Gd2d4] }) (4. Nc3 { Just as good: your queen knight heads out first. } 4... g5 5. d4 g4 { About level, and your knight on f3 is hit. [%cal Rg4f3] }) (4. d3 { Nearly as good by the numbers, but your pawn stops on d3. } 4... g5 5. h4 g4 { About level, but you have no pawn centre: your d pawn stayed on d3. [%csl Rd3] }) 4... g5 { He guards his pawn on f4 with the g pawn. Against a knight move, your own knight would come to c3. Now hit the guard: your h pawn goes two squares. [%cal Yh2h4,Rg5f4] } 5. h4 { Your h pawn hits g5, the guard of f4. [%cal Gh4g5] } (5. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight comes out and lets his pawn attack first. } 5... g4 6. Bxf4 gxf3 7. Qxf3 { About level: you gave a knight for two pawns, but your pieces are out and your pawn centre stands. [%csl Gd4] }) (5. g3 { Just as good: your pawn hits f4 instead. } 5... g4 6. Nh4 f3 { About level: his pawns push on, and your knight sits on the edge. [%csl Rh4] }) (5. Bc4 { A little worse: his g pawn jumps at your knight. } 5... g4 6. O-O gxf3 { Slightly better for him: your knight is gone for his pawn. [%csl Rf3] }) (5. h3 { Nearly as good, though it does not hit g5. } 5... h6 { About level, but his pawns on f4 and g5 stand firm. [%csl Rg5] }) 5... g4 { His pawn hits your knight, and his d pawn guards e5, so the knight cannot jump there. Send it home. [%cal Yf3g1,Rd6e5] } 6. Ng1 { Home again, and safe. Against a queen move, do not take on f4: bring a knight out. Against f5 or a knight move, your bishop can take it. [%cal Gg1e2] } (6. Ne5 { The jump to e5 works when he has no pawn on d6. Here it does not. } 6... dxe5 { His d pawn took your knight for nothing. Better for him. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Ng5 { Nearly as good, but his f pawn chases the knight at once. } 6... f6 7. Bxf4 fxg5 8. Bxg5 { Slightly better for him: you have two pawns and a big centre for the knight. [%csl Rg5] }) (6. Bxf4 { Taking f4 lets his pawn take your knight. } 6... gxf3 7. Qxf3 Nc6 { Better for him: a knight for a pawn, even with your pieces out. [%csl Rf3] }) (6. Nfd2 { The knight steps back, but it blocks your bishop. } 6... Nf6 7. Nc3 Nh5 { Better for him: his knight guards f4, and your knight on d2 blocks your dark bishop. [%cal Rh5f4] }) (6. Nc3 { Developing, but your knight on f3 is left to his pawn. } 6... gxf3 7. Qxf3 { Better for him: his pawn took the knight, and your queen got one pawn back. [%csl Rf3] }) (6. Nh2 { The knight goes to h2, and his queen takes h4 with check. } 6... Qxh4+ 7. Kd2 Qf2+ { Better for him: his queen is loose in your camp, and your king is in trouble. [%cal Rf2d2] }) 6... Bh6 { His bishop guards f4, a shade less strong than his queen move to f6. Now bring your queen knight out. [%cal Yb1c3,Rh6f4] } 7. Nc3 { The knight guards e4 and your pawn centre. Against a knight move, your other knight comes to e2 just the same. [%cal Gc3e4] } (7. Ne2 { The other knight first is a little worse: e4 has no guard yet. } 7... Qf6 8. Nbc3 Ne7 { About level: his queen and knight gang up on your centre. [%csl Re4] }) (7. Bc4 { Nearly as good: the bishop comes out, though e4 has no guard yet. } 7... Qf6 { About level: his queen hits your centre, and your queen knight is still at home. [%cal Rf6d4] }) (7. Bd3 { The bishop guards e4, but it blocks your queen. [%cal Bd3e4] } 7... Qf6 8. Nc3 Ne7 { About level: your pieces stand in each other's way. [%csl Rd3] }) (7. Qd3 { The queen steps up too early. } 7... Qf6 8. Nc3 Ne7 { Slightly better for him: his queen and knight come out with ease. [%csl Rd3] }) (7. Be2 { A quiet move, and his f pawn strikes. } 7... f5 8. exf5 Qe7 { Slightly better for him: your pawn centre broke up. [%csl Re4] }) (7. g3 { The pawn hits f4 too early. } 7... f5 8. Nc3 fxe4 { Slightly better for him: he took e4, and your centre is gone. [%csl Re4] }) 7... c6 { His c pawn keeps your knight out of d5. Bring your king knight back out to e2. [%cal Yg1e2] } 8. Nge2 { That is the pawn centre: your pawns own the middle, and both your knights are out. His pawn on f4 has only his bishop to guard it. About level. [%cal Ge2f4,Gc1f4] } (8. Qd3 { Nearly as good: the queen steps up and guards the centre. } 8... Qf6 9. Nge2 Na6 { About level: your knight still comes to e2 a move later. [%csl Ge2] }) (8. Bd3 { Nearly as good: the bishop guards e4. [%cal Bd3e4] } 8... Qf6 { About level, but your king knight is still at home. [%csl Rg1] }) (8. h5 { A little slow: the pawn push wins no time. } 8... f5 9. exf5 Qe7+ { About level: his f pawn broke into your centre, and his queen checks. [%csl Re1] }) (8. Bd2 { A quiet move that leaves f4 alone. } 8... Nf6 9. Nge2 Nh5 { About level: his knight on h5 holds f4 for good. [%cal Rh5f4] }) (8. Bc4 { The bishop comes out, but his pawn gets ready to hit it. } 8... Nf6 9. g3 d5 { Slightly better for him: his pawn hits your bishop and breaks into your centre. [%csl Rd5] }) (8. e5 { Pushing too soon gives away your pawn centre. } 8... dxe5 9. dxe5 Qxd1+ 10. Nxd1 { Better for him: the queens came off, and your pawn on e5 is weak. [%csl Re5] }) 1-0


