{ Gambit accepted: his extra pawn stands on f4, and your knight is out on f3. }
3... Be7 { His bishop comes to e7 and eyes h4. Build your game: what is your move? [%cal Re7h4] } 4. Bc4 { The bishop comes out and aims at f7. Against a knight move to f6, your king pawn would kick it. [%cal Gc4f7] } (4. Be2 { Just as good: the bishop comes out on the quiet side. } 4... d5 { Level: he strikes in the middle at once. [%csl Rd5] }) (4. Qe2 { Just as good for now, though your queen blocks your bishop. } 4... d5 { About level: his pawn hits your e pawn, and your queen stands in front of your bishop. [%csl Re2] }) (4. Nc3 { Developing, but it lets his bishop check on h4. } 4... Bh4+ 5. Ke2 d5 { About level, but your king walked to e2. [%csl Re2] }) (4. d4 { The centre push lets his bishop check you. } 4... Bh4+ 5. Kd2 d6 { About level, but your king had to step to d2. [%csl Rd2] }) (4. d3 { Just as good by the numbers, but his bishop checks. } 4... Bh4+ 5. Ke2 d5 { About level, but your king stands on e2 and blocks your bishop. [%csl Re2] }) (4. h4 { Too slow: the pawn push wins nothing. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 O-O { Slightly better for him: he castled, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rf4] }) (4. Bd3 { The bishop blocks your d pawn. } 4... Nf6 { Slightly better for him: he develops freely, and your bishop on d3 blocks your d pawn. [%csl Rd3] }) 4... Bh4+ { His bishop checks you from h4. How do you answer? } 5. Kf1 { The king steps aside, and his bishop on h4 hits nothing. Against d5, your bishop would take it. [%cal Rh4e1] } (5. g3 { Blocking with the pawn lets his f pawn take. } 5... fxg3 6. O-O gxh2+ { About level, though he is three pawns up: his pawns are deep in your camp. [%csl Rh2] }) (5. Ke2 { Your king steps forward to e2, into the open. } 5... c6 6. Nxh4 Qxh4 { Slightly better for him: his queen is active, and your king stands in the open. [%csl Re2] }) (5. Nxh4 { Taking the bishop lets his queen check. } 5... Qxh4+ 6. Kf1 Ne7 { Slightly better for him: his queen is active, and you cannot castle. [%csl Rf1] }) 5... d6 { He opens a road for his light bishop with d6. What now? [%cal Rc8g4] } 6. d4 { The pawn centre: your two middle pawns stand side by side. Had his bishop come to e6, you would take it. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (6. Nc3 { Just as good: your knight guards e4 first. [%cal Bc3e4] } 6... Be7 { Level: his bishop drops back, and you can still build the pawn centre. [%csl Gd4] }) (6. d3 { Just as good, but your pawn stops on d3. } 6... Bf6 7. Nc3 Be6 { About level, but you have no pawn centre. [%csl Rd3] }) (6. c3 { A little slow. } 6... Qf6 7. d4 Ne7 { About level: his queen guards f4, and your king still stands on f1. [%cal Rf6f4][%csl Rf1] }) (6. Bb3 { The bishop steps back for no reason. } 6... Bg4 7. d4 Qf6 { Slightly better for him: his queen and bishop are active. [%csl Rf4] }) (6. Nxh4 { Taking the bishop lets his queen in. } 6... Qxh4 7. Qe1 Qf6 { Slightly better for him: his queen guards f4, and you are still a pawn down. [%csl Rf4] }) (6. Qe1 { The queen hits his bishop, but his bishop takes her. } 6... Bxe1 { His bishop took your queen on e1: lost for you. [%csl Re1] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen steps up, slower than the pawn centre. } 6... Bg4 { Slightly better for him: his bishop pins your knight to your queen. [%cal Rg4e2] }) 6... Bg4 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen. What now? [%cal Rg4d1] } 7. Bxf4 { Your bishop takes the gambit pawn back: material is level. Against a knight move, your own knight comes to c3. [%csl Gf4] } (7. Nc3 { Just as good: the knight comes out first. } 7... Ne7 8. Bxf4 Ng6 { Slightly better for you: you took the pawn back a move later. [%csl Gf4] }) (7. Be2 { The bishop breaks the pin but blocks your queen. } 7... Qf6 { Level: his queen guards f4, and the pawn stays his. [%csl Rf4] }) (7. Qd3 { The queen steps up, but f4 stays his. } 7... Nc6 8. Bb5 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 { About level: a knight and a bishop came off, and f4 is still his. [%csl Rf4] }) (7. c3 { A quiet move, and f4 stays his. } 7... Qf6 8. Qb3 Nd7 { About level: his queen guards f4. [%cal Rf6f4] }) (7. Nxh4 { Taking the bishop loses your queen. } 7... Bxd1 { His bishop took your queen on d1. Lost for you. [%csl Rd1] }) (7. Bxf7+ { The bishop grabs f7 with check, but it is lost. } 7... Kxf7 8. Bxf4 Ke8 { Better for him: you gave a bishop for two pawns, and his king is safe. [%csl Re8] }) (7. Be3 { Developing, but his f pawn takes the bishop. } 7... fxe3 { His f pawn took your bishop. Better for him. [%csl Re3] }) 7... Qf6 { His queen hits your bishop on f4 and your pawn on d4. What now? [%cal Rf6f4,Rf6d4] } 8. Be3 { The bishop steps back to e3 and guards d4. Material is level, and your pawn centre stands firm. [%cal Ge3d4][%csl Ge4] } (8. h3 { Just as good: the pawn asks his bishop to decide. } 8... Be6 9. Bxe6 Qxf4 { Slightly better for you: he took back f4, but material is level and your pawn centre stands. [%csl Rf4] }) (8. g3 { The pawn guards your bishop, a little less good. } 8... Nc6 { Level: your bishop is safe, but his knight comes out and hits d4. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (8. Qd2 { The queen guards the bishop, but he trades on f3. } 8... Bxf3 9. gxf3 g5 { Slightly better for him: your king side pawns are broken. [%csl Rf3] }) (8. Nc3 { Developing, but your bishop on f4 hangs. } 8... Qxf4 9. Nd5 Qxe4 { Better for him: his queen took your bishop and then your e pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (8. Qd3 { The queen steps up, but your bishop on f4 hangs. } 8... Qxf4 { His queen simply took your bishop. Better for him. [%csl Rf4] }) (8. e5 { Pushing past leaves your bishop on f4 hanging. } 8... Qxf4 9. Qd2 Qxd2 10. Nbxd2 { Better for him: he took your bishop and swapped queens. [%csl Rd2] }) (8. Bg5 { The bishop hits his queen, but his bishop takes it. } 8... Bxg5 9. Nc3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 { Better for him: he won a whole piece. [%csl Rf3] }) (8. Nbd2 { Developing, but your bishop on f4 still hangs. } 8... Qxf4 { His queen took the bishop you left on f4. Better for him. [%csl Rf4] }) 1-0


